    "id": "testicular-changes",
    "title": "Testicular Pain, Lumps and Swelling: Knowing When to Act",
    "category": "Sexual fitness",
    "art": "rose",
    "excerpt": "Which changes should prompt a routine appointment or urgent help?",
    "body": "<p><strong>Sudden severe testicular pain needs emergency assessment. Pain with nausea or vomiting, or pain that continues while resting, can also require immediate help. Do not wait to see whether a home maneuver, pain medicine or a later appointment makes the question easier.</strong> NHS guidance identifies testicular torsion among the possible urgent causes. <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/testicle-pain/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 1: NHS\">[1]</a></p>\n<p>For less acute changes, such as a new lump or swelling, arrange a clinical assessment. The aim is to identify the cause, not to diagnose yourself from a description or decide that embarrassment makes the concern less important.</p>\n<h2 id=\"use-the-speed-and-severity-of-the-change\">Use the speed and severity of the change</h2>\n<p>Tell the service when the pain or swelling began, whether it was sudden, and whether there was an injury or another relevant event. Mention fever, urinary symptoms, nausea, abdominal pain or feeling unwell.</p>\n<p>NHS guidance distinguishes sudden severe pain from other symptoms that still need prompt review. Different causes can overlap in how they feel, so a familiar explanation such as “I must have pulled something” should not override an acute warning sign. <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/testicle-pain/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 1: NHS\">[1]</a></p>\n<p>If emergency help is needed, arrange appropriate transport rather than driving while severely unwell. In the US, call 911 for an emergency or attend an emergency department as appropriate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-lump-deserves-assessment-without-a-predicted-diagnosis\">A lump deserves assessment without a predicted diagnosis</h2>\n<p>Scrotal lumps and swelling can have several causes. Many are not serious, but a new lump, a change in testicular shape or a swelling should be checked. NHS guidance recommends clinical review rather than relying on self-diagnosis. <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/testicle-lumps-and-swellings/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 2: NHS\">[2]</a></p>\n<p>You can describe a location or change in ordinary words. Do not repeatedly squeeze or manipulate the area to determine whether it matches an online account. A clinician can consider which structures are involved and whether examination, imaging or other tests are appropriate.</p>\n<p>The absence of pain does not establish that a lump can be ignored. Equally, the presence of a lump does not establish cancer. Assessment is the useful step between those assumptions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"explain-what-is-happening-to-function-and-comfort\">Explain what is happening to function and comfort</h2>\n<p>Mention whether the concern affects walking, sitting, urination, erections, ejaculation or sexual contact. These details can help the clinician understand the impact without turning sexual performance into the measure of how serious the problem is.</p>\n<p>If there has been relevant sexual contact, discharge or concern about an STI, include it. Infection testing is a clinical question, not a requirement to give a detailed personal history beyond what is useful for care. <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/std/treatment-guidelines/chlamydia.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 3: CDC\">[3]</a> <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/std/treatment-guidelines/gonorrhea-adults.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 4: CDC\">[4]</a></p>\n<p>You can ask what information the clinician needs and why. If a question feels difficult, saying “I am unsure how to describe that” is a reasonable beginning.</p>\n<h2 id=\"know-what-to-ask-about-the-examination\">Know what to ask about the examination</h2>\n<p>An intimate examination should be explained. Ask what the clinician proposes, whether a chaperone is available, and how to pause or stop if needed. Needing urgent assessment does not make understandable communication unimportant. <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.gmc-uk.org/professional-standards/the-professional-standards/intimate-examinations-and-chaperones/intimate-examinations-and-chaperones\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 5: General Medical Council\">[5]</a></p>\n<p>Tell the service about accessibility, communication or trauma-related needs that may affect the visit. You can ask whether an ultrasound or another investigation is planned and what it is intended to answer. Do not assume that one person's previous workup predicts exactly what your assessment will involve.</p>\n<h2 id=\"keep-symptom-guidance-separate-from-a-screening-ritual\">Keep symptom guidance separate from a screening ritual</h2>\n<p>This article is about noticing and acting on symptoms. It does not prescribe a universal cancer self-screening schedule for adults without symptoms. If your history calls for particular surveillance, ask your clinician what is recommended and why.</p>\n<p>Becoming familiar with your own body can help you describe a change. It does not require repeated measurements, photographs or comparison with someone else's anatomy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"leave-with-the-next-decision-clearly-stated\">Leave with the next decision clearly stated</h2>\n<p>This original Vulpes Ignis checklist records what was assessed, any tests arranged, expected result timing, symptom instructions and who to contact if the problem changes.</p>\n<p>If the symptom persists or worsens despite an earlier reassuring visit, contact the service again and describe the change. A prior assessment is part of the history, not a permanent explanation for every future symptom. You deserve a clear route to care and a chance to ask questions without shame.</p>",
